#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# region_dimension

#% text
# `region_dimension(reg)` is the region's own dimension: 0 for points, 1 for curves, 2 for surfaces, 3 for solids.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# A degenerate primitive is the set it actually is: a triangle with equal or collinear corners is the segment between its outermost corners, a tetrahedron with coplanar corners the convex polygon they span, a polygon whose corners lie on one line a segment, a line with equal ends, or a disk, ball, circle or sphere of radius 0, a point.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
[region_dimension(circle()), region_dimension(disk()), region_dimension(ball()), region_dimension(point([1, 2]))]
#% output
#= [1, 2, 3, 0]

#% text
# Degenerate primitives have the dimension of the set they are:

#% cell
[region_dimension(triangle([[0, 0], [1, 1], [2, 2]])), region_dimension(tetrahedron([[0, 0, 0], [1, 0, 0], [0, 1, 0], [1, 1, 0]])), region_dimension(ball([0, 0, 0], 0))]
#% output
#= [1, 2, 0]

#% text
# An equation takes one dimension away:

#% cell
region_dimension(implicit_region(x^2 + y^2 + z^2 == 1, [x, y, z]))
#% output
#= 2

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `region_embedding_dimension`
